#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048593 :topic/id #uuid "ac718bb5-4681-5ba0-883a-072801cad492" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054810 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054455 :question/id #uuid "67ce214a-e9e9-5a32-b48c-d9488cb141ac" :question/math-academy-id "e-134" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ when $f(x)=x^3$." :question/worked-solution "The power rule states that if\n\n$$\nf(x) = x^n\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf'(x) = nx^{n-1}\n$$\n\nFor the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=x^3\n$$\n\nwe have $n=3$. So\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= 3 \\cdot x^{3 - 1} = 3x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32dd6880-3725-564a-9e85-0d39b6960c72" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Power Function Using the Power Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054811 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054456 :question/id #uuid "54c18c81-9deb-5958-81a0-1abecf91ba58" :question/math-academy-id "e-135" :question/problem "Calculate the derivative of $f(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$." :question/worked-solution "Here, we can rewrite the function as\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\dfrac{1}{x^2} = x^{-2}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with $n=-2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= (-2) \\cdot x^{-2 - 1} \\\\\n&=-2x^{-3} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{x^{3}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2ff1808-d239-5625-93b7-21a98ada3fab" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Negative Power Function Using the Power Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054812 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054457 :question/id #uuid "1717a513-6866-5b4e-8c9a-1ea7f343acab" :question/math-academy-id "e-136" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ when $y=\\sqrt{x}$." :question/worked-solution "Writing the radical as a rational exponent, we have\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}=x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nApplying the power rule with\n\n$$\nn=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{1/2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{1/2 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}x^{-1/2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8d06cd8-9aae-50ab-90dc-6c70b545d569" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Fractional Power Function Using the Power Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054465 :question/id #uuid "27baaab1-2088-5277-be24-5dfba2bfa5f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-137" :question/problem "Find $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 12$." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is constant, its derivative is zero. Therefore, $f'(x)=0$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "654f8868-880b-53dc-ba4c-677aecb10b95" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Constant Function Using the Power Rule"}] :topic/math-academy-id 35 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049481}] :topic/title "The Power Rule for Differentiation"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049592 :topic/difficulty 0.7391803218132104 :topic/id #uuid "02ecb448-e544-51fa-b80e-63ee84a4cef1" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166813 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166651 :question/id #uuid "cafdc3de-f065-5a60-be3e-8521a49929d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8459" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166652 :question/id #uuid "a2564a21-1cf4-572e-a33b-b8db4aa60dfa" :question/math-academy-id "e-8460" :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166653 :question/id #uuid "c71175ba-2700-5007-84ec-6bd68c14263a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8461" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1" :knowledge-point/title "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3215 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050608}] :topic/title "Introduction to Differential Equations"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271 :topic/difficulty 1.313241991049613 :topic/id #uuid "3b427f21-e66e-500e-b1b5-ede02904d802" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092260 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092116 :question/id #uuid "c986ab8f-a8a6-5721-bc8d-ba7ec19b95ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-727" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = e^{5x +4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = e^u, \\qquad u=5x+4.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5{e}^{u} \\\\  & =5{e}^{5x+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a83f86ab-a008-5487-b118-f1495a0fe4d6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Linear Exponent"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092261 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092118 :question/id #uuid "775dfc95-df31-5198-aeb2-4603b8386957" :question/math-academy-id "e-728" :question/problem "Given that $y= 3\\cdot 2^{1 - x},$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 3\\cdot 2^u, \\qquad u=1-x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = 3\\cdot 2^u\\ln(2),\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-1.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {2}^{1-x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f4bfd24-dafd-57b3-940a-154a6e0acf3d" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a General Exponential Function Using the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092263 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092119 :question/id #uuid "b15a88b5-e2c4-51c1-90d5-031c2a8c23d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-729" :question/problem "Given that $y=e^{-x^2},$ calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = e^u, \\qquad u=-x^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = e^u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} =-2x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (-2x) \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{u} \\\\  & =-2x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1007 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050400}] :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Exponential Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050690 :topic/difficulty 0.8054939588520751 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428108 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 1116->278. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048932} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "5f59a4ad-6d31-5757-be65-f24e070b2c60" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096613 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096413 :question/id #uuid "6624681a-bb00-59db-8df9-4d5a3cd00434" :question/math-academy-id "e-3627" :question/problem "Given that $y=5\\ln x$, find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of the natural logarithm is the reciprocal function:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{ \\textrm{d} x}\\left( \\ln x \\right)= \\dfrac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nWe apply our formula and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}   & = \\left(5\\ln x\\right)'\\  \n                                                    & = 5 (\\ln x)'\\\n                                                    &=5\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x}\\\n                                                    &= \\dfrac{5}{x}.\n\t\t\t\t\t\t  \n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a71301e9-bc7f-53b9-900e-c3bd37f96b03" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Natural Logarithm Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096614 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096414 :question/id #uuid "58d47fb0-cf75-58c4-8eea-f3ae97333159" :question/math-academy-id "e-3628" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2\\ln x+x^2-1$, find $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\tf'(x) &= \\left(2\\ln x+x^2-1\\right)'\\\n\t\t\t&=2 \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x}+2x-0\\\n\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{2}{x}+2x.\n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce3c28-857b-5538-b362-cf52526e3688" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum Containing a Natural Logarithm Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096615 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096415 :question/id #uuid "f61ac550-d62c-558f-b6d5-0d4c9aba8089" :question/math-academy-id "e-3629" :question/problem "Given that $y=4\\log_3 x,$ find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply our formula for the derivative when the base of the logarithm is a number other than $e.$ By doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\t\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}&=\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(4\\log_3 x\\right)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t &=4\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(\\log_3 x\\right)\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t &=4\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x \\ln 3}\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&=\\dfrac{4}{x \\ln 3}.\n\n}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5856f3cc-5423-5a97-b2fd-7f44e0797ec7" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function With a General Base"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096616 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096416 :question/id #uuid "5eaa00f8-0fd0-5de6-a7c0-965db6de3a5c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3630" :question/problem "Given that $y= \\log_4 x + \\ln x$,  find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the slope of the tangent at a given point, we have to find $\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left( \\log_4 x + \\ln x \\right)\\\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\log_4 x) + \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\ln x )\\\n\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{x\\ln 4} + \\dfrac{1}{x}.\n}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+\\frac{1}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cef7fc21-23f4-5d6a-a208-9963ef23c784" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line of a Logarithmic Function at a Point"}] :topic/math-academy-id 1116 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050902}] :topic/title "Differentiating Logarithmic Functions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050789 :topic/difficulty 0.9971443699008332 :topic/encompasses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186428836 :encompassing/rationale "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 305->1686. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." :encompassing/topic {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050985} :encompassing/weight 1.0}] :topic/id #uuid "671f0549-be03-552c-a435-a204b8c7e733" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064281 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064125 :question/id #uuid "2bb17470-6365-5e08-b649-f9f42146a4c6" :question/math-academy-id "e-732" :question/problem "Calculate $f'(x)$ if $f(x) = 2\\sin (3x - 1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function\n\n$$\nf = 2\\sin u, \\qquad u=3x-1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 2\\cos u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "73216188-6524-55c3-b945-86d13ef64170" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064282 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064126 :question/id #uuid "7f56b401-c800-5708-8978-b84ce3f542f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-733" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ if $y = 4\\csc (5x + 3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = 4 \\csc u, \\qquad u = 5x+3.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = -4\\csc u\\cot u, \\qquad\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{csc}⁡u\\operatorname{cot}⁡u \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{csc}⁡(5x+3)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(5x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ef81a0d-5a3a-5d7c-83e9-7d9ed0e8c5c6" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Reciprocal Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064283 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064127 :question/id #uuid "373a63fc-3dae-5880-8a49-63b5c64888f3" :question/math-academy-id "e-734" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\sin^3{x}$, calculate $f'(x).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write\n\n$$\nf(x) = (\\sin{x})^3.\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $f(x)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nf = u^3, \\qquad u=\\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d u} = 3u^2, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos{x}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =3(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ec11481-cea2-514b-aa78-707fcf8838cc" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Power of a Trigonometric Function Using the Chain Rule"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064284 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064129 :question/id #uuid "4ebd0289-b278-5352-b7bd-10ae218c43fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-735" :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y = \\tan{\\left(\\sin(x)\\right)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny = \\tan u, \\qquad u = \\sin x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d u} = \\sec^2 u,\\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm d x} = \\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡u\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a483e892-90f2-51f4-80cd-2b1200cd0c37" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Composition of Trigonometric Functions Using the Chain Rule"}] :topic/math-academy-id 305 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048776} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050400}] :topic/title "The Chain Rule With Trigonometric Functions"}]}
